WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.777
A payments job adds up each merchant's payments.

2
00:00:05.777 --> 00:00:10.585
A million payments sit in four partitions, and one map task reads each one.

3
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:19.020
Merchant 42 has payments in all four, and they must meet in one task: a reduce task, which owns a share of the merchants.

4
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:26.388
A map task finds each row's owner: hash the merchant id, divide by four, and keep the remainder.

5
00:00:26.388 --> 00:00:27.811
Merchant 42 gets 3.

6
00:00:28.033 --> 00:00:30.838
Streaming rows straight to their owners breaks.

7
00:00:30.838 --> 00:00:34.895
An owner may not be running yet, or it may die and lose what it got.

8
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:42.701
So each map task writes first: one file on its own disk, sorted by owner, with one block per reduce task.

9
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.938
Then each reduce task fetches its block from every file.

10
00:00:46.938 --> 00:00:50.039
Reduce task 3 now holds all of merchant 42.

11
00:00:50.267 --> 00:00:53.992
Four map tasks and four reduce tasks make 16 fetches.

12
00:00:53.992 --> 00:00:56.381
A thousand of each make a million.

13
00:00:56.567 --> 00:01:00.623
Every row is written to disk, read back and sent over the network.

14
00:01:00.623 --> 00:01:04.495
That's the shuffle, the most expensive step in most Spark jobs.

15
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.447
Now join each payment to its merchant.

16
00:01:07.447 --> 00:01:09.833
The merchants table has 500 rows.

17
00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:13.881
One way: shuffle the merchants too, with the same rule.

18
00:01:13.881 --> 00:01:18.217
Merchant 42's row lands in reduce task 3, beside its payments.

19
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:23.591
Each reduce task sorts both sides and walks them together: a sort-merge join.

20
00:01:23.591 --> 00:01:26.873
It works at any size, but it moves every payment.

21
00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:29.749
The other way leaves the payments in place.

22
00:01:29.749 --> 00:01:35.495
The driver, the process running the job, sends a copy of the small table to every map task.

23
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:40.152
Each task builds a hash table from it and joins its payments right there.

24
00:01:40.152 --> 00:01:43.628
That's a broadcast hash join, and no payment is shuffled.

25
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.967
It ran in about half the time.

26
00:01:45.967 --> 00:01:51.600
But every machine holds a copy, so Spark only does this by itself below 10 MB.

27
00:01:51.767 --> 00:01:54.112
Shuffle both sides when both are big.

28
00:01:54.112 --> 00:01:56.900
When one side is small, send it to the data.
